WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.300
Hi, I'm Chris Manning here to answer your&nbsp;
questions on artificial intelligence.&nbsp;

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:14.920
[Music] Here are a couple&nbsp;
of AI-generated questions.&nbsp;

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:20.320
Considering the rapid progress in NLP what&nbsp;
key factors and actions should the community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.520
prioritize for responsible and ethical AI&nbsp;
language model deployment and development?&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.280
Well it's really good to know that&nbsp;
the AI systems are also thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:36.360
about what's important for responsible&nbsp;
and ethical AI language model deployment.&nbsp;

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:43.480
So, there are lots of things to think about there.&nbsp;
We certainly want systems that don't exhibit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:49.920
conventional gender biases or ethnic stereotypes.
The fact of the matter is that all of the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:56.480
large language models do exhibit those problems.
In part it's because these models are just trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.320
on a lot of historical data from the world.
You know, one of the the things that human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:07.320
beings are better at is having in their&nbsp;
head how they think the world should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:12.320
rather than just knowing how the world has&nbsp;
been and having statistics based on that.&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.920
But there are also details of the machine learning&nbsp;
models where they tend to emphasize majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.560
cases and forget about the minority cases.
So we should be working out how to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:30.160
models that do a better job of that. But we should&nbsp;
also be trying to get data from different sources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.080
so it represents different groups.
And we should have the preferences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.200
of different groups involved in the&nbsp;
evaluation of models. finding their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:45.160
weak spots, teaching them how to behave.
So a couple of people at Stanford have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:50.680
been involved in this idea of jury learning,&nbsp;
Professor Michael Bernstein and his students,&nbsp;

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:57.000
and the idea there is that there isn't sort of&nbsp;
one right answer to how you should behave in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.920
many contexts, that different subgroups&nbsp;
of people have different Impressions.&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.360
And so you actually want to represent&nbsp;
a jury of your peers and how different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:12.760
people feel about things, and so lots&nbsp;
of things to explore in those areas.&nbsp;

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.400
AI system asks: what are some of&nbsp;
the most promising and exciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:24.240
real world applications of NLP that you&nbsp;
foresee emerging in the next few years?&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:31.920
Uh, tons! So I think we're going to see a lot&nbsp;
more NLP systems being deployed very successfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:39.120
everywhere. So a lot of websites already have&nbsp;
a little AI assistant in the bottom corner.&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.080
I think we're going to see a new generation&nbsp;
of ones powered by large language models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.960
which really work much more fluently.
I think we're going to see much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:58.080
use of conversational assistants. So you're&nbsp;
thinking about how to word an email to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:03.240
boss, or you're not quite sure what&nbsp;
you should say to your boyfriend.&nbsp;

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.680
It turns out that you can&nbsp;
get advice, drafting help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:12.920
suggestions of what to say from a large language&nbsp;
model that are actually useful these days.&nbsp;

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:17.920
You know, I think there's just a whole world&nbsp;
of ways that we can use these models because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.680
our world is so language based and that we're&nbsp;
all just sort of exploring what some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.680
possibilities are.
[Music]

